// Title: Potential COM Object Hijacking Via TreatAs Subkey - Registry
// Sigma ID: 9b0f8a61-91b2-464f-aceb-0527e0a45020
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Kutepov Anton, oscd.community
// Date: 2019-10-23 | Modified: 2025-10-26
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, persistence, T1546.015
// Description: Detects COM object hijacking via TreatAs subkey
// False positives:
//   - Maybe some system utilities in rare cases use linking keys for backward compatibility
// References:
//   - https://bohops.com/2018/08/18/abusing-the-com-registry-structure-part-2-loading-techniques-for-evasion-and-persistence/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_persistence_com_key_linking.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where (RegistryKey endswith "HKU*" and RegistryKey endswith "Classes\\CLSID*" and RegistryKey contains "\\TreatAs") and (not(InitiatingProcessFolderPath =~ "C:\\WINDOWS\\system32\\svchost.exe"))
